  <!-- Real operational note instead of generic setup tips -->
  <h2>Two Things About Holafly Most Pages Don't Mention</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Their China eSIM has a VPN built in.</strong> You don't install a separate VPN app or buy a subscription before you fly &mdash; Google Maps, WhatsApp, and Gmail just work on the Holafly connection. That's a genuine point of difference if China is on the itinerary, since sorting out a VPN in advance is one of the more annoying parts of that trip for most travelers.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:.75rem;">
    <strong>Cancel a Plans subscription and you're not fully cut off.</strong> Holafly's "Always On" tier drops you to 1GB a month across 160+ destinations instead of zero &mdash; enough to land somewhere, get a taxi, and get online, without paying for a full plan you're not using between trips.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:.75rem;">
    Unlimited also doesn't mean unmanaged: fair-use limits can slow speeds after very heavy same-day use on some plans, and hotspot support varies by plan rather than being guaranteed across the board &mdash; check the specific plan page if tethering a laptop is the point of the trip.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:.75rem;">
    One practical habit: add a buffer day or two to a destination eSIM's length rather than cutting it exactly to your trip. Flights get delayed and the plan expires on schedule regardless of whether you actually landed on time.
  </p>
  <p style="margin-top:.75rem;">
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply beyond Holafly specifically, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide. We've also written a more in-depth <a href="https://www.adamandlinds.com/blog/holafly-discount-code-esims-and-plans/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">field-tested Holafly write-up</a> on our travel site covering where we've actually used it, for anyone who wants the longer version.
  </p>

